<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suez Crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Suez Crisis was the international crisis of 1956 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Israel invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> nationalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>. Under pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the invaders withdrew. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> treat it as a humiliation that showed Britain could no longer act as an independent imperial power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suez-crisis.jpg" alt="Suez Crisis" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fleet Air Arm official photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_Said_from_air.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Suez Crisis</b> of 1956 delivered the public humiliation that confirmed what financial reality had already established: when President Eisenhower's administration refused support for sterling and blocked British access to IMF funds unless Britain withdrew from Egypt, Prime Minister Anthony Eden's climbdown demonstrated that Britain could no longer…</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…build post-war institutions: Lester B. Pearson won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1957 for proposing the United Nations Emergency Force during the <b>Suez Crisis</b>, which set the pattern for armed peacekeeping, and the lawyer John Peters Humphrey prepared the first draft of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its forces fought in Korea, in colonial conflicts in Malaya, Kenya and Cyprus, at <b>Suez</b> in 1956 and in the Falklands War of 1982, and were deployed in Northern Ireland from 1969 to 2007.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Suez Crisis</b> Nasser gained great popularity among the masses of the Arab world, and pan-Arabism reached its height with Nasserism and Ba’athism in the 1950s and 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>…Silahi&quot; was first a nationalist song, written by Salah Jahin, set by Kamal Al Taweel and sung by Umm Kulthum during the <b>Suez Crisis</b> of 1956, known in Egypt and the Arab world as the Tripartite Aggression, when Egypt was invaded by the United Kingdom, France and Israel.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wallah-zaman-ya-silahi">Wallah Zaman, Ya Silahi</a></li><li><blockquote>Citing ill health, Eden stepped down two months after the <b>Suez Crisis</b>, in which Britain and France had been forced by American pressure to withdraw from Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
